in the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean scores for the two tests are not equal.\n\n*Hint: Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1))))$.\n\n*You may use the fact that $P(T > 2.042) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $30$ degrees of freedom.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c9f3db-2a93-5f7b-bb55-1994a95061ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Two Means: Applications" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048611 :topic/math-academy-id 4118 :topic/title "Confidence Intervals for Two Means: Unequal and Unknown Population Variance"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98dea7a9-60ba-5981-93e7-55a2bcb5e90c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191356} {:answer/id #uuid "9acd9f20-f8d6-5016-9dcb-816be3bd7ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191357} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f86591-200f-504b-893f-b1288ce949e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191358} {:answer/id #uuid "098ad6d6-c61c-5641-a562-63d6a2976549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191359} {:answer/id #uuid "3c90b87b-9fd1-5b96-b010-302cdce31d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191360}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f738a90d-5b94-5ad3-8f3f-be7892fe4e70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191355}] :question/id #uuid "c81f0e50-f6c4-5fd0-82ab-0b24feffc44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-224431" :question/problem "A restaurant owner believes that the mean number of customers per day has changed since he changed the menu. The number of customers per day after and before the menu change are known to be normally distributed with means $μ_{1}$ and $μ_{2}$. The table below shows the summary statistics of several randomly selected days before and after the menu change, where $n$ is the number of days in each sample, $x^(―)$ is the mean number of customers per day, and $s^{2}$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | After Menu Change | Before Menu Change |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $x^(―)$ | $190$ | $150$ |\n| $s^{2}$ | $20^{2}$ | $30^{2}$ |\n\nAssuming that the samples are independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the mean number of customers are approximately $40 ± 30.364$.\n2. All values within the $95%$ confidence interval are larger than $10$.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean number of customers per day has increased by at least $10$ since the menu change.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $1.833$ | $2.262$ | $4.297$ |\n| $10$ | $1.812$ | $2.228$ | $4.144$ |\n| $11$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $4.025$ |\n| $12$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $3.930$ |\n| $13$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $3.852$ |\n\n*Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1))))$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e8873a-8f80-50de-a6c1-2775f9d10bd9" :knowledge-point/title "Diagrams Containing Many Triangles" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049432 :topic/math-academy-id 4065 :topic/title "Combining Similarity Criteria for Triangles"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3436963-b400-5249-988c-83f275dab849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191583} {:answer/id #uuid "bdbfe361-f266-5d45-8dab-6fe18c6edff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191584} {:answer/id #uuid "31831c23-40c0-5add-afb3-6aee23508eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191585} {:answer/id #uuid "45386c0c-09b5-52b0-988e-a7719634c920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191586} {:answer/id #uuid "5a890332-235b-5358-b4c0-c839ad88ba02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191587}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0f66e267-dbe1-530a-8353-bfa326f66d41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191582}] :question/id #uuid "4a05ac98-40d