db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383540} :answer-field/id #uuid "164ed81b-856e-50e1-9161-0818ce673900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7c80401-6b6e-5da5-9047-45e3ded9ecb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161828" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible on $U.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=-{f}^{′}(a).$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$ which could be smaller than $U.$ \n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={x}^{2}$ satisfies the conditions of the inverse function theorem for $U=(-2,2)$ and $a=1.$ However, $f(x)$ is not invertible on the entire set $U,$ only on a subset of $U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/math-academy-id 4149 :topic/title "The Inverse Function Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8040ed28-477e-573d-b3d0-8c2d9139a9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194334} {:answer/id #uuid "a09347b4-024f-56c1-b29f-940e03afb175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194335} {:answer/id #uuid "c42e35bc-917f-5446-9a95-6f1532136e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194336} {:answer/id #uuid "e796ab40-baae-50de-bb21-d8eb721d099d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194337} {:answer/id #uuid "2386ec89-b532-583b-9415-8f913c2abc19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194338}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1289ab05-6968-52bb-84fa-24c928b2b008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194333}] :question/id #uuid "68f89203-1bc9-5c52-b5b9-6a6702702838" :question/math-academy-id "q-161829" :question/problem "![](images/95/95aee22f12b54815fd65343f1b9817a7301f44d4a2b3a87446cade23a290941a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$, whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x = 2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x = 4$. By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n1. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x = 1$\n2. $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y = 1$\n3. The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = 5.2$ equals $(1)/(f^{′}(4))$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048592 :topic/math-academy-id 4254 :topic/title "Truth Sets of Predicates"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d9f9d38-34d5-5e60-8bad-93b429a077cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194833} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3c4358-6ee8-56f5-8169-ffdf5eb0ab71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194834} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e091ee-714d-5783-9dc4-6912f3b2f5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194835} {:answer/id #uuid "4239faf3-c0fa-57fc-9de5-8c1ebc7e61d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194836} {:answer/id #uuid "46d3454e-3fa3-5eb5-aac7-a301fcc25b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194837}] :answer-field/id #uuid "277fc0e3-02ca-54ea-a184-55e3d313f7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-fiel