ed9b0e-e772-5f3f-9113-c537b55b3749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200810} {:answer/id #uuid "90b5b2b6-e7af-5ee1-8997-5c76799f60c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200811} {:answer/id #uuid "a050ae9d-56db-50aa-bc11-0d932c0b27a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200812} {:answer/id #uuid "53025a82-f881-55b5-bd90-124de6d345d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200813} {:answer/id #uuid "5d114da3-56ea-5c52-ab80-e5411a9db1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200814}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2abbf727-1c63-5e9e-8369-4a2c18b8729c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200809}] :question/id #uuid "0823b313-2833-55dd-9ded-3ea5b3134af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-223787" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d998b155d5639ae5993430e317ad725e15a0fda27da9bd1efa3e5678d6aa27cb.png)\n\nThe scores of two particular geometry and algebra tests are known to be normally distributed with equal variance.\n\nA teacher suspects that the average grade in the geometry test is higher than the average grade in the algebra test. So, they independently and randomly sampled $n_{1} = 15$ students who took the geometry test and $n_{2} = 20$ students who took the algebra test, finding that their mean scores are $x^(―)_{1} = 76$ points and $x^(―)_{2} = 70$ points, respectively, and the pooled variance is $s_{p}^{2} = 4^{2}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $98%$ confidence interval for the difference between the average scores are approximately $6 ± 3.334$.\n2. All values contained within the $98%$ confidence interval are larger than $3$.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests the geometry mean score is at least $3$ points higher than the algebra mean score.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.44) = 0.01$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $33$ degrees of freedom, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204001 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88aeb74c-2879-5677-98b2-add447352925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Two Means: Applications" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050435 :topic/math-academy-id 4946 :topic/title "Confidence Intervals for Two Means: Equal and Unknown Population Variance"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4b1da93-4385-54f2-8716-e60983a38f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200840} {:answer/id #uuid "f37c2ce3-eca3-5b47-8c9b-09bba30c602a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200841} {:answer/id #uuid "40cc65a4-c362-5481-98c9-6eea9d3f701e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200842} {:answer/id #uuid "68c8e954-9118-51d2-b98a-bf5440684ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200843} {:answer/id #uuid "73b2b2a2-03aa-521a-b787-4f20ac9caba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200844}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c81b8be9-5ce1-56ff-8a19-47dabc39a37e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200839}] :question/id #uuid "bc9338c1-cb81-5239-8882-74da0e832df7" :question/math-academy-id "q-224346" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9448614876f83aff8a755a95dcd76323efefc50b516b2c9142e29d57c30f203.png)\n\nThe diameters of balls for bearings produced by machines X and Y are known to be normally distributed with equal variance. Engineers want to verify the claim that the average diameter of balls produced by machine X is not equal to the average diameter of balls produced by machine Y.\n\nThey independently and randomly sampled $n_{1} = 22$ balls produced by machine X and $n_{2} = 20$ balls produced by machine Y, finding that their mean diameters are $x^(―)_{1} = 20.09$ millimeters and $x^(―)_{2} = 19.85$ millimeters, respectively, and the pooled variance is $s_{p}^{2} = 0.1^{2}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $96%$ confidence interval for the difference between the average diameters are approximately $0.24 ± 0.065$.\n2. The $96%$ confidence interval does not contain $0$.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests the mean diameter of balls produced by machine X is not equal to the mean diameter of balls produced by machine Y.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.12) = 0.02$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $40$ degrees of freedom, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61d1f878-e9e7-59c3-adb3-907b1fa1c77f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Principal Disjunctive Normal Forms" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049447 :topic/math-academy-id 5396 :topic/title "Principal Disjunctive Normal Forms"}]}]